2. TEMPO — https://github.com/hpcbg/tempo
   Licence: MIT (declared in TEMPO's README; the repository ships no LICENSE
            file, so the README declaration is the authority relied on here).

ADAPTED CODE (derived work, substantially modified):

  wisepack_ws/src/wisepack_bringup/topics.py and qos.py
      Adapted from tempo_ws/src/tempo_bringup/{topics,qos}.py.
      Reused: the "one canonical contract module" pattern, the argument that
      DDS QoS *is* the robustness layer rather than application heartbeat code,
      the single-writer discipline, and the profile-selection helper that makes
      requested/offered QoS mismatches impossible by construction.
      All topics, types and profiles are WISEPACK's.

  scripts/lib_validate.sh, scripts/clean_dds_shm.sh
      Adapted from TEMPO's scripts of the same names. The orphaned-shared-memory
      problem under `--ipc=host` and the `ros2 topic echo` QoS traps are TEMPO's
      findings.

  Dockerfile.wisepack, build_wisepack_ws.sh, run_vulcanexus_wisepack.sh,
  run_wisepack_dashboard.sh
      Adapted from Dockerfile.tempo, build_tempo_ws.sh, run_vulcanexus_tempo.sh
      and run_tempo_dashboard.sh. Reused: Docker-only operation, mounting the
      repo at its own absolute path, the apt-vs-pip pytest/pluggy workaround,
      baking fastapi into the image against venue wifi, and the live/sim
      dashboard split.

  web/app.py, web/ros_observer.py, web/index.html
      Adapted from tempo/web/{app.py,ros_observer.py,index.html}.
      Reused: the FastAPI + in-memory cache + REST/WebSocket structure, the
      position-free topology graph whose coordinates are derived in the
      frontend, the "KPI read from the newest results/ artefact, shown as
      'not measured' until it exists" pattern, and the two-theme design-token
      set. WISEPACK's dashboard additionally COMMANDS (approval is the point of
      the project), which TEMPO's deliberately does not.

